/* No per-question timecodes: review marks the paper and prints the script. */
const ANSWER_TIMES={};

/* ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
   ║  EDIT PER TEST · TRANSCRIPT  —  see BUILD_INSTRUCTIONS.md §5c           ║
   ║  {t,sp,tx}=speech · add answers:[id],aw:"substring" on answer lines ·   ║
   ║  {t,type:"break",label:"..."}=divider. Cover the whole audio.          ║
   ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝ */
const TRANSCRIPT=[
  {type:"break",label:"Part 1 · Questions 1–10"},
  {sp:"Narrator",tx:"Barrington Historical Buildings Tours"},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Good morning, Barrington Tourist Information. This is Tom speaking. How can I help you?"},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Oh, good morning. I'm ringing to find out about the historical buildings tours. I heard you have a special week when places are open to the public. It's in September, isn't it?"},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Usually, but we've had to move it to the thirteenth of October because a few of the places couldn't open in September. We've been holding it for three years now. It's basically a celebration of the history of the town."},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Right. I came to Barrington when I was a little girl, once, with my parents, but I can't remember much about it. It's really pretty old, isn't it?"},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Yes, it's one of the oldest towns in this area. The earliest record we have is the year 1803.",aw:"1803",answers:[1]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Right, so it's well over two hundred years old. And there was an old flour mill, I remember."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"That's right. That was quite an important industry in the early days, and they also produced furniture in a factory down by the river, although that has been demolished now.",aw:"furniture",answers:[2]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Oh, I didn't know about that. Anyway, can you tell me a little about the tours?"},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Sure. We visit a number of historical buildings which are specially open for the tour. So we start off in the centre of town at what used to be the Town Hall. That was built in 1841, and then in the 1950s they moved to new premises, and ever since then it's been used as a library. It still has a lot of the original features, including the grand staircase.",aw:"library",answers:[3]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"OK. I don't really remember that."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Well, you'll be shown all around, and then we'll move on down the road to another building which was built in 1882, and that's the original school. That's just down Main Street. I don't know if you remember it.",aw:"school",answers:[4]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"I'm not sure."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"It's a one-storey stone building set back from the road. It's still in use, though of course it's been considerably extended. Then I don't know if you remember the big house in the town square, Cornell House."},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"I remember a big house with pillars in front."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"That's it. It used to be the home of the Cornell family, but at present it's a bank. The family sold it back in the 1950s. The upper floors aren't usually open to the public, but we have special permission to take you all around on the tour.",aw:"bank",answers:[5]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Interesting. And one place I remember visiting when I was a child was a farm outside town. I think the family who owned it were Quakers. The main thing I remember about it was that they didn't have any electricity there. It was all lit with oil lamps. But I suppose all that's changed now.",aw:"electricity",answers:[6]},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"No, that's still the case. And in fact that's the final place we visit on the tour. It's interesting seeing all the old equipment."},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Ah, great."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Now, as well as the tours themselves, we have a lot of other attractions in the town that week. For example, you can buy produce from the local farmers at a market in the main square every day that week.",aw:"market",answers:[7]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Lovely."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"And if you're here on the Saturday evening, there's a special event which is a dinner. It's all held by candlelight, and the staff all wear historical costumes, just to get into the theme.",aw:"dinner",answers:[8]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Yes, well, I'd been thinking of just coming for the day, but I think we might make a weekend of it. But what about accommodation? That would be just for the Saturday night."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Well, some places are booked up, but there's a place you could try which might have a room. That's called Barrett Lodge, spelled B-A-R-R-E, double T.",aw:"Barrett",answers:[9]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"OK. Do you have a phone number for them?"},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Oh yes, it's 5297238126.",aw:"5297238126",answers:[10]},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Right. Well, thanks for all your help."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Hope you enjoy your visit."},
  {sp:"Woman",tx:"Bye."},
  {sp:"Tom",tx:"Goodbye."},
  {type:"break",label:"Part 2 · Questions 11–20"},
  {sp:"Narrator",tx:"Buying Holiday Accommodation"},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Welcome, everyone, and thanks for coming to this information evening. My name is Brad Warren, and I'm here to tell you about our wonderful new investment opportunity: a chance to buy a villa in the Silverton Resort in the magnificent Bunna Bunna National Park."},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"First, let me tell you about the history of the Silverton Resort. The Silverton family has lived in this beautiful national park for over a hundred years. They settled here back in 1904. Until now, they were the only private landowners in the national park, and they established a hotel here in 1911. The Bunna Bunna area was not declared a national park until 1907, three years after the Silverton family's arrival.",answers:[11]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Today the park is home to some of the oldest trees in Australia, but it's renowned for its diverse range of bird life. There are over 160 species here. While we don't have the high waterfalls that other parks can boast of, we do have other attractions, such as our treetop walking trails and wonderful caves to explore.",answers:[12]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"So what is so special about our villas? The architects did a fantastic job blending the villas into the natural environment and making sure that they are energy efficient, as they're all equipped with solar panels. But I believe what makes them truly unique is the fact that only a select few villas will ever be built, so not many people will get the opportunity to live here.",answers:[13]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Once you've bought your villa, you don't need to worry about doing the gardening, because as part of your contract we take care of that for you. And for a small additional fee, when you're not using your villa, you can have a secure income by letting us organise short-term holiday rentals for it.",answers:[14]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Now let me tell you about some other features of the villas. We offer a choice of two designs, and all of our villas are nestled in the mountains. It can get a bit cold up here, so Type A villas feature a fireplace for those cold winter nights. The Type B villas are located in the same area, but are much larger. These villas have the living area downstairs and three bedrooms upstairs. There's a large family-size bathroom upstairs too.",answers:[15]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Now, I'm sure you're keen to find out more about the details of this fantastic investment. So if you could all turn to the map of the resort, I'll tell you about the location and surroundings of the villas. If you look at the map, you can see the villas on the right at the bottom. To the left of the villas, you'll see a long, low building with a fig tree next to it. That's our art gallery. You'll find some fine landscape paintings there by local painters.",answers:[16]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Between the villas and that pond is a very old building which was once the Silverton family home. It's now a day spa, offering hair and beauty treatments. There are so many advantages of living in a resort like this one. Imagine strolling over to our award-winning restaurant from your villa. Just walk past the pond, and it's there in front of you. As you can see, the restaurant faces the pond, so you can sit and look at the water, which is lit up at night.",answers:[17, 18]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"The first building that most visitors see when they arrive at the resort is the hotel, because it's opposite the car park. That's it in the top right-hand corner of the map.",answers:[19]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Now, is there anything I've forgotten? Ah yes, the children's play area, very important for the little ones, and very near the villas. Walk from the villas towards the picnic area, and you'll come across this area where you can sit under the fig tree and watch the kids play.",answers:[20]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"So I hope that gives you a much better idea of where everything is located. Now, I'm sure you've all got plenty to think about and discuss, so I'll close here and let you- [recording cuts off]"},
  {type:"break",label:"Part 3 · Questions 21–30"},
  {sp:"Narrator",tx:"Autonomous Underwater Vehicles"},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Hi, Henry. Did you understand the lecture about autonomous underwater vehicles?"},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"AUVs, you mean? Partly. I know that AUVs are underwater vehicles without drivers, and that they can be programmed to do certain actions underwater, like taking photos. But in the last lecture, when the professor moved on to AUV designs of the future, I got a bit confused."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Me too. Have you time to talk about it now?"},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Sure. I've got my notes here. The first AUV design he talked about was the S83."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Yes. My notes say it's been designed to operate in challenging conditions."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Challenging means really deep water, I suppose."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Not necessarily. In the case of the S83, I think the lecturer was referring to the sea floor, which is often really uneven, you know. It can be hilly and bumpy."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"My notes say that the S83 will have retractable wheels, as well as a special balancing mechanism to help it cope with that.",answers:[21]},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Okay, that's clear. Next, the Sea Explorer. I've put that it's been designed to operate continually for months or even years, but it can't operate at extreme depths."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"That's right, because they're working on using rechargeable power from the sun.",answers:[22]},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"I guess it can't go too far down, then. But doesn't the Wave Rider use an alternative energy source?"},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"No. Its design is energy efficient, but that's because it's smooth and streamlined. In fact, it looks just like a bullet, and that helps it to glide through the water easily, using minimal power.",answers:[23]},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"That sounds amazing. Ah, okay, one more: the T602. It's designed to locate obstructions that might cause a hazard, like shipwrecks and pipelines."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Can't the current AUVs do this?"},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Yes, but T602 will be able to detect structures beneath the sea floor, which is pretty amazing.",answers:[24]},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Yeah, I think- [recording cuts off]"},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"There are so many. I wonder how scientists choose which underwater vehicle to use."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Some vehicles are obviously better suited to particular jobs and conditions, but maybe it simply depends on how much money is allocated to the project."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"I remember the lecturer saying that, while cost and conditions are important, it's actually the type of scientific information needed that's the main concern.",answers:[25]},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"That makes sense. For our homework, we had to find a scenario where an underwater vehicle's been used and discuss the research it's generated. Did you find anything?"},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Yes, I did. It was to do with the investigations of suspicious oil off the coast of California some years ago."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"I remember reading about it. It was quite a big deal. Lots of theories were suggested, like the source of the oil was simply [unclear] leaking into the water from recreational boats in the area."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Yes, that was the opinion voiced in the media, but scientists proposed that car and truck emissions running into our waterways were the cause.",answers:[26]},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"A tanker had been spotted nearby, but there had been no suggestion that it was to blame for the oil."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"So they decided to use an underwater vehicle called Alvin to find the answers. Did they choose Alvin because it was built to work in these warmer seas off the coast?"},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Actually, they weren't sure how it would cope under those conditions. This was a while ago, and back then Alvin was considered cutting-edge and had the most high-level capabilities. It could be pre-programmed to take samples. Photographic and scanning capability didn't come until later, of course, with the newer AUVs.",answers:[27]},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"I see. I didn't realise that. What did Alvin's samples tell the scientists?"},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Well, they managed to use Alvin's findings to work out the amount of oil that had soaked into the floor of the sea. They tried to make assumptions about the chemical make-up of the oil and the length of time it had already been in the seabed, but back then they just didn't have the know-how.",answers:[28]},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Back in Alvin's time, I suppose scientists found it more challenging to monitor the effects of oil on the underwater environment."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Yes. They were particularly concerned about how to gauge the effect on sea animals."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Yes, but it's still quite difficult now, even though scientists can investigate underwater areas freely using a range of AUVs. Oil is such a complicated mixture of lots of different chemicals.",answers:[29]},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"That's true. It reacts differently depending on the type of marine life in each location and, of course, things like water temperature, tides and currents."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Must be hard for scientists, then."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"I think they quite enjoy studying natural oil seeps, though."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Ah, oil seeps. That's where oil has escaped naturally from a crack or hole in [unclear] of the Earth and spread through the water."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"Yes, rather than oil from a tanker accident, for example."},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"So why do you think scientists enjoy studying them? Is it because they aren't as dangerous to work on as the accidental oil spill? I imagine the scientists would need less protection."},
  {sp:"Student 2",tx:"It's more that scientists actually view oil seeps as being useful because they give us an insight into how marine life can adapt, you know, through years of living in an area where there is constant exposure to oil.",answers:[30]},
  {sp:"Student 1",tx:"Even though researchers obviously get a lot out of their work in this field, I think more money needs to be spent on helping them investigate natural oil seeps."},
  {type:"break",label:"Part 4 · Questions 31–40"},
  {sp:"Narrator",tx:"Life on the International Space Station"},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"For my presentation, I'm going to summarise the findings of a research project on life aboard the International Space Station in the early days of its operation: its teething problems, as it were. I'll start by giving a little background information."},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"The space station crew were essentially a constantly changing team of scientists from different countries who lived on the space station for several months at a time. And they conducted a number of experiments, including some studies of living conditions in space, which is what I'm going to be looking at now."},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"So what was life like on board in the very confined conditions of the space station? Well, the early impressions recorded by the astronauts were very different from what people had expected. They'd expected that the air would smell stale or artificial, but the astronauts found that it was fresh. So that was one very positive finding.",aw:"fresh",answers:[31]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"And although they were in such a confined space and people worried that it would get very hot, in fact they found that it was very easy to turn the temperature up or down in order to keep it within a comfortable range."},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"The feedback on the food was also positive. Astronauts on previous space stations had described the meals as boring, but the International Space Station meals were praised for their variety. So that was another good piece of feedback.",aw:"variety",answers:[32]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"And something which is taken for granted today, but was a key morale booster in those days, was communication between the space station and Earth, which the astronauts said was clear and reliable. So in the early days, it seems that the mood was overall very positive.",aw:"communication",answers:[33]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"However, after a few days, the difficulties and frustrations became clearer. Above all, the crew complained about the constant noise caused by the air filters, which used to rattle and buzz twenty-four hours a day, interrupting their sleep.",aw:"sleep",answers:[34]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"As well as that, the astronauts complained that the Velcro fasteners, which were positioned all over the station, didn't work well, and so a lot of the time objects weren't secure in the cabin, but floated about."},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"A particularly serious problem was that the systems used on the space station had been developed in a number of different countries, and they didn't always work well together. For example, it was impossible to mix the water produced from different systems because it contained different sorts of preservatives, and these reacted together and blocked the pipes.",aw:"water",answers:[35]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Another problem was connected to repairs. At the best of times, these took up an inordinate amount of time on the station, and the crew complained that many of the tools that they'd been provided with weren't suitable for the jobs they had to do, so they had to improvise, which meant that jobs took a lot longer than they needed to.",aw:"tools",answers:[36]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Another related problem was to do with access to equipment that needed maintaining, because the instructions on the equipment were often located in places where they were difficult to read, like on the back of equipment. It was often difficult or impossible to read them.",aw:"instructions",answers:[37]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"So, having identified these problems, the researchers came up with a number of suggestions about how conditions could be improved on subsequent missions. First, it became clear that, although there had been a great deal of consultation with the crews, the systems and equipment on the space station needed much more wide-ranging testing.",aw:"testing",answers:[38]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Then the whole issue of language training came under review. Astronauts from different countries had to be able to talk to the controllers on Earth and to understand them. Now, although they'd had extensive language training, the astronauts sometimes had problems comprehending what was said because they weren't familiar with the accent of the speaker, and this sometimes led to quite dangerous misunderstandings.",aw:"accent",answers:[39]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"Finally, and this is the point I found most surprising, the researchers felt that the designers of the space station were paying too much attention to safety rather than considering operating issues: that is, how the station could be run comfortably and smoothly. And the recommendation was to make changes here at quite a fundamental level.",aw:"safety",answers:[40]},
  {sp:"Presenter",tx:"So those were the main points I covered in the- [recording cuts off]"},
];

